## Why preventive

`/security-audit` runs at pre-handoff and catches bad patterns before deploy — but by then the bad patterns are baked in across the codebase. These rules apply *during* development so violations are rare to begin with. Each rule is enforced by either a `/wire-check` grep, an Edge Function helper, or a Tech Lead review point.

---

## Trust boundaries (companion to backend.mdc + tech-spec §10b)

Every binding in the Wiring Map declares a Trust mode: `RLS-direct`, `Edge-validated`, `Webhook`, `Service-role-only`. The selection rule:

| Concern | Mode |
|---|---|
| User reads/writes their own data | `RLS-direct` |
| Money, credits, paid features | `Edge-validated` (server checks balance, server grants) |
| LLM / paid external API | `Edge-validated` (key is server-only) |
| Cross-user reads (feeds, leaderboards) | `Edge-validated` (Edge applies row limits / safe filters) |
| External callbacks (payments, email events) | `Webhook` (signature + idempotency) |
| Cron / batch / admin | `Service-role-only` (never client-callable) |

A binding without a declared Trust mode is a security defect — `/wire-check` flags it.

